hrmpa"],["he","בְּהַ֣ר",null,"H2022","HR/Ncmsc","b"],["he","צִיּ֔וֹן",null,"H6726","HNp"],["he","לִשְׁפֹּ֖ט",null,"H8199","HR/Vqc","l"],["he","אֶת",null,"H853","HTo"],["he","הַ֣ר",null,"H2022","HNcmsc"],["he","עֵשָׂ֑ו",null,"H6215","HNp"],["he","וְהָיְתָ֥ה",null,"H1961","HC/Vqq3fs","c"],["he","לַֽיהוָ֖ה",null,"H3068","HR/Np","l"],["he","הַמְּלוּכָֽה",null,"H4410","HTd/Ncfsa","d"]]},"gl":{"H2377":"vision","H5662":"Obadiah","H3541":"thus","H559":"to say","H136":"Lord","H3069":"YHWH/God","H123":"Edom","H8052":"tidings","H8085":"hear","H854":"with","H3068":"LORD","H6735":"envoy","H1471":"nation","H7971":"depart","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H5921":"upon","H4421":"battle","H2009":"behold","H6996":"small","H5414":"give","H959":"to despise","H859":"you(m.s.)","H3966":"much","H2087":"arrogance","H3820":"heart","H5377":"to deceive","H7931":"to dwell","H2288":"cleft","H5553":"rock","H4791":"height","H7675":"seat","H4310":"who?","H3381":"to go down","H776":"country/planet","H518":"if","H1361":"to exult","H5404":"eagle","H996":"between","H3556":"star","H7760":"make","H7064":"nest","H8033":"there","H5002":"utterance","H1590":"thief","H935":"come","H7703":"to ruin","H3915":"night","H349":"how?","H1820":"to cease","H3808":"not","H1589":"to steal","H1767":"sufficiency","H1219":"to gather/restrain/fortify","H7604":"to remain","H5955":"gleaning","H2664":"to search","H6215":"Esau","H1158":"to enquire","H4710":"treasure","H5704":"till","H1366":"boundary","H3605":"all","H376":"man","H1285":"covenant","H3201":"be able","H7965":"peace","H3899":"food","H4204":"net","H8478":"under","H369":"nothing","H8394":"understanding","H3117":"day","H1931":"he/she/it","H6":"to perish","H2450":"wise","H2022":"mount","H2865":"to to be dismayed","H1368":"mighty man","H8487":"Teman","H4616":"because","H3772":"cut","H6993":"slaughter","H2555":"violence","H251":"male-sibling","H3290":"Jacob","H3680":"to cover","H955":"shame","H5769":"enduring","H5975":"stand","H5048":"before","H7617":"to take captive","H2114":"be a stranger","H2428":"Helech","H5237":"foreign","H8179":"gate","H3389":"Jerusalem","H3032":"to cast a lot","H1486":"allotted","H1571":"also","H259":"one","H408":"not","H7200":"see","H5235":"misfortune","H8055":"to rejoice","H1121":"child","H3063":"Judah","H1431":"to magnify","H6310":"lip","H6869":"dearth","H5971":"people","H343":"calamity","H7451":"harmful","H6563":"plunder","H853":"[Obj.]","H6412":"survivor","H5462":"to shut","H8300":"survivor","H3588":"for","H7138":"near","H834":"which","H6213":"do","H1576":"recompense","H7725":"return","H7218":"head","H8354":"to drink","H6944":"holiness","H8548":"continually","H3886":"to swallow","H1961":"to be","H6726":"Zion","H6413":"survivor","H3423":"possess","H1004":"place","H4180":"possession","H784":"fire","H3130":"Joseph","H3852":"flame","H7179":"stubble","H1814":"to burn/pursue","H398":"to eat","H1696":"speak","H5045":"Negeb","H8219":"Shephelah","H6430":"Philistine","H7704":"Sirion","H669":"Ephraim","H8111":"Samaria","H1144":"Benjamin","H1568":"Gilead","H1546":"captivity","H2426":"rampart","H2088":"this","H3478":"Israel","H3669":"Canaanite","H6886":"Zarephath","H5614":"Sepharad","H5892":"excitement","H5927":"rise","H3467":"to save","H8199":"to judge","H4410":"kingship"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Edom. The Jews understand this of the Romans; others apply it to themselves; but it seems to speak of the Idumeans. — Ambassador, prophet, or angel; or God has suffered the passions of men to act. All is here animated. God appears leading on the various nations. (Calmet) — He directed their thoughts to unite against Edom. (Worthington) — Yet he did not approve of their ambition. (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Contemptible. The nations of Chaldea, &c., were far more potent. Yet Edom must be brought still lower."],["haydock",3,null,"jerome","Rocks. Hebrew, “Selah,” or Petra, the capital. (Calmet) — People dwell in caverns from Eleutheropolis to Ailath. (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Down. (Job xx. 6.) How vain is all human power!"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Cluster. (Jeremias xlix. 9.) The Chaldeans take all, and remove the people, ver. 7."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Peace. The enemy had used their services against the Jews; but now they invade their confederates. (Calmet) — Of this the Idumeans were not aware. (Haydock)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Esau. Job and Eliphaz were both from this country."],["haydock",9,null,"jerome","South. Hebrew, “Theman,” (Calmet) where the Romans kept a garrison, fifteen miles south of Petra. (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Slaughter. They invaded the dominions of Achaz, and incited the enemy to destroy all, 2 Paralipomenon xxviii. 17., and Psalm cxxxvi. 7."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Captive. He alludes to the taking of Sedecias. — Lots, for the booty, or whether they should burn the city or not. All was regulated by lots. (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Thou shalt not look, &c., or thou shouldst not, &c. It is a reprehension for what they had done, and at the same time a declaration that these things should not pass unpunished. (Challoner) — God admonishes, and at the same time insinuates that the Idumeans would act quite the reverse. (Worthington) — Magnify. Literally, thou shalt not speak arrogantly against the children of Juda, as insulting them in their distress, (Challoner) like people mocking. When they shall be themselves afflicted, they shall cease to upbraid the Jews. (Calmet)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Army. Hebrew also, “goods.” Septuagint, “thou wilt not join their army in the day of ruin.” (Haydock) — Thou wilt have other things to think about."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Flee. The Idumeans might easily have concealed the fugitives. But they were so inhuman as to fall upon them, (Calmet) or drive them back."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Nations around. (Haydock) — Josephus ([Antiquities?] x. 11.) does not specify Edom. But the prophets had announced their destruction, effected by Nabuchodonosor, while the main part of his army besieged Tyre. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Drink. They shall rejoice at your fall, as you did at that of Juda; (St. Jerome) or, as my people has not been spared, can you expect to escape? (Jeremias xlix. 12.) — Not. These nations and the Chaldeans themselves were brought low, while the Jews regained the regal power. The cup denotes vengeance, Psalm lxxiv. 9. Plautus uses the same expression: ut senex hoc eodem poculo, quo ego bibi, biberet. (Casina.) (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"theodoret","Sion. This prosperity, in the historical sense, was promised to the Jews, after their return; and they enjoyed as much of it as their sins did not hinder; the rest was fulfilled in Christ. (St. Jerome, ad Dard.) (Worthington) — Holy. Providence watched over the Jews in a particular manner, while the neighbouring nations fell a prey to the Persians, to Alexander, &c. The persecution of Epiphanes was sharp, but of short duration; and it gave occasion to the Jews to regain their liberty, and to have kings (Calmet) little (Haydock) inferior to those of old. — Them. Hebrew, “its goods.” The Jews obtained all Palestine. (Calmet) — Christ extends his dominion over the world. (Theodoret)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Stubble. The Jews often attacked Edom, and at last forced them to submit to the law of circumcision. — Remains. Septuagint copies vary; “corn fire, or carrier.” (Haydock) — Those who escaped alone from battle had the former title. (Hesyc. purphoros.) (Calmet) — Priests went with fire before the armies engaged. If they were slain, it was a sign, that no quarter was given, as these were accounted sacred. (Grabe, Prol.) (Haydock)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Plains. Sephala, Josue x. 40. — Samaria, which the Cutheans had occupied, till Alexander subjected them to the Jews, and Hircan asserted his authority. (Josephus, Antiquities xiii. 18.) — Galaad, east of the Jordan. Benjamin alone did not occupy this country."],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Sarepta. This was accomplished after the persecution of Epiphanes. — Bosphorus. So St. Jerome’s master interpreted Bispharad. But it seems rather to mean a part of Mesopotamia. Sippara stands above, where the Euphrates divides its streams."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Saviours, the Machabees, as figures of Christ. The temporal power of the Jews ended thirty-seven years after his resurrection. (Calmet) — The rest of this prophecy (ver. 19.) regards Christ’s kingdom over all nations, which believe in him, and receive the remission of their sins, Acts x. 43. (St. Augustine, City of God xviii. 31.) (Worthington)"],["challoner",12,null,"challoner","Thou shalt not look, etc.... or, thou shouldst not, etc. It is a reprehension for what they had done, and at the same time a declaration that these things should not pass unpunished.—Ibid. Thou shalt not magnify thy mouth.... That is, thou shalt not speak arrogantly against the children of Juda as insulting them in their distress."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"],"theodoret":["Theodoret of Cyrus",0,1,457,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/14570a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]},"fa":[[1,null,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 5",0,339,"[Passage quoted, Obad. 1.] THE Lord God has heard a report from the Lord. And this report was about the call of the Gentiles."],[12,null,1,"Concerning Repentance","Book 2",1,397,"… And first grant that I may know how with inmost affection to mourn with those who sin; for this is a very great virtue, since it is written: \"And thou shall not rejoice over the children of Judah in the day of their destruction, and speak not proudly in the day of their trouble. Grant that so often as the sin of any one who has fallen is made known to me I may suffer with him, and not chide him proudly, but mourn and weep, so that weeping over another I may mourn for myself, saying, \"Tamar hath been more righteous than I.\""],[15,null,2,"The Christian Topography","Book 5",2,550,"This is Obadiah (Abdiou) the fourth in order, who also was privileged to prophesy concerning the mystery regarding Christ, and who speaks thus: Because the day of the Lord is near upon all the nations. This taken in its obvious meaning is spoken of the Scythians, that is, of Gog and Magog, but it is most properly applicable to the Lord Christ."],[17,null,2,"The Christian Topography","Book 5",2,550,"for the prophet shortly afterwards says: But on Mount Zion there shall be salvation."]],"fau":[["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339],["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397],["cosmas-indicopleustes","Cosmas Indicopleustes",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0]],"ftr":[["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"],["J. W. McCrindle, Hakluyt Society (1897)"]]}